# Rule 2 of the product direction (issue #3; QUESTIONS Q16): nothing moves until the person docks something.
# Story: three windows are open when Arbor starts. They stay where they are, at the sizes they have, whatever happens on
# the Mac, until their owner docks one. Then that one moves, and nothing else.
# A resume is a start: it finds the windows that opened while Arbor was not looking. So is `relaunch`. The very first launch
# is in `PromisesTests` and in the chaos lab, which can open windows before Arbor has ever run.
quiet
pause
open Safari 800x600
open Notes 400x300
open Mail 640x420
move 102 1000 100 400 300
move 103 760 440 640 420
resume
expect still
expect count docked 0
expect floating 101
expect floating 102
expect floating 103
expect size 101 800x600
expect at 102 1000 100
expect at 103 760 440
# What apps and macOS do by themselves is no reason to move anything.
open Maps 500x400
expect still
expect floating 104
expect at 104 300 200
focus 101
expect still
minimize 102
expect still
restore 102
expect still
hide Mail
expect still
unhide Mail
expect still
title 101 Another page
expect still
close 104
expect still
wait 30
expect still
# Nor is looking.
home
expect still
show-all
expect still
desktop
expect still
relayout
expect still
# Arbor is quit and started again with nothing docked: neither moves anything.
relaunch
expect still
expect count docked 0
expect at 102 1000 100
expect at 103 760 440
# The first dock: that window, once. The others were not in its way and stay.
dock 101 left
expect moved-once
expect docked 101
expect still 102
expect still 103
expect at 102 1000 100
expect at 103 760 440
